          "source_quote": "αὐτοὶ αὐτὰ φυγαδεύοντες καὶ ἱκετευόμενοι ὑπ’ αὐτῶν",
          "english_quote": "The neuter αὐτά can collect both the demons and the demonic sufferings just mentioned, whereas the masculine αὐτῶν most naturally refers specifically to the demons.",
          "issue": "The note misleadingly contrasts αὐτά with a supposedly gender-distinguishing αὐτῶν. The genitive plural αὐτῶν has the same form in all genders. Taking its referent as the demons is contextually defensible, especially because they can implore the hearers, but the inflection does not establish that referent. The main English pronouns remain defensible.",
          "suggested_revision": "Revise the note to explain that αὐτά may refer to the sufferings or collectively to the preceding demons and sufferings, while αὐτῶν is formally compatible with any gender. Identify supplication, rather than morphological gender, as the reason for preferring demons as the latter pronoun’s referent. Preserve the alternative reference and submit the revised note for fresh review.",
