          "source_quote": "ἡ τοῦ θεοῦ χεὶρ εἰς ἴασιν αὐτοῦ ἐπιψαῦσαι δυνηθῇ ὡς ἁγνοῦ καὶ πιστεύοντος",
          "english_quote": "Although the pronoun follows a reference to the body, the masculine participles ἁγνοῦ and πιστεύοντος identify the referent as the afflicted person.",
          "issue": "ἁγνοῦ is an adjective, not a participle. Both ἁγνοῦ and πιστεύοντος have forms shared by masculine and neuter genitive singulars, so morphology alone does not distinguish the afflicted person from a metonymic reference to the body. The personal referent is defensible from context, especially the attribution of believing, but the note overstates its grammatical certainty. The preceding assertion that αὐτοῦ belongs to ἐπιψαῦσαι also leaves undisclosed its possible attachment to ἴασιν.",
          "suggested_revision": "Correct the grammatical description: ἁγνοῦ is an adjective and πιστεύοντος a participle, and both forms permit masculine or neuter agreement. Explain that the main rendering chooses the afflicted person on contextual and semantic grounds, while a metonymic reference to the body remains possible. Also acknowledge αὐτοῦ with ἴασιν ('for his healing') as an alternative to construing it with ἐπιψαῦσαι ('touch him'). The main wording need not change solely because of these alternatives; the revised disclosure requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
